#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078149 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "20b289d4-96a7-5d1b-9714-6afabab4804d" :knowledge-point/title "Further Determining Rates of Change in Geometrical Settings"}] :question/id #uuid "a3234c09-08eb-56f2-8a4c-d6430d38c356" :question/math-academy-id "e-275" :question/problem "A spherical balloon is being inflated so that its volume is increasing. Find the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing with respect to the radius at the moment when the diameter of the balloon is equal to $12\\, \\text{cm}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/620/Source/Images/038c730db46c27977ecaf5b36ce200bc.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we are given that the diameter of the balloon is $12\\, \\text{cm}$, its radius is\n\n$$\nr =6\\, \\text{cm}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that the volume of a sphere with radius $r$ is given by\n\n$$\nV= V(r) = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\n$$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $V(r)$ with respect to $r$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} V'(r) &= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d r}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi r^3\\right) \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\pi (3r^2) \\\\[5pt] &= 4\\pi r^2, \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $r=6$ gives\n\n$$\nV'(6)= 4\\pi (6)^2= 144\\pi\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the volume of the balloon is changing when its diameter is $12\\, \\text{cm}\\,$ is $\\, 144\\pi \\, \\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$.\n\n**Note:** We read $\\text{cm}^3/\\text{cm}$ as \"centimeters cubed per centimeter\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078150 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "3c4f9912-4b08-5e11-8ef6-4e4ee35765c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-276" :question/problem "Lizzie buys $b$ pounds of bananas at a price $p$, in dollars, that can be modeled by the function $p(b)=5e^{-0.2b}$. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is equal to $p'(5)$.\n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(b) &= \\frac{d}{db}(5e^{-0.2b}) \\\\\n&= 5e^{-0.2b} \\cdot (-0.2) \\\\\n&=-e^{-0.2b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $b=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np^{′}(5) &= -e^{-0.2(5)} \\\\\n&=-e^{-1} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{e}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate change of the price when $b=5$ pounds is\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{e}\n$$\n\ndollars per pound."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/id #uuid "f2eda994-e00a-5e9b-a26d-720d01e6fa07" :question/math-academy-id "e-6174" :question/problem "A particle moves in a straight line. After $t$ seconds the particle is $\\, f(t)=2t^3 - 5t + 9\\,$ feet away from its initial position. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position as a function of $t$?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is equal to $f'(t)$.\n\nWe take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(2t^{3} - 5t + 9) \\\\\n&= 6t^{2} - 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $t$ seconds, the instantaneous rate of change of the particle's position is $6t^2 - 5$ feet per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dff5ec0b-1456-5231-94e1-0fc5923d6672" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3ea3a0-0252-57e3-aeec-232b99135da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262338} {:answer/id #uuid "96f05212-4e3a-599e-92ef-43d04918b0f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ meter per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262339} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3f02ef-59c7-5837-a6c2-9d92c02c90d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262340} {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb16e8-cf8a-5273-9f9e-97b4fbe73eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262341} {:answer/id #uuid "0445507a-2423-5178-bf12-706b5c412c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ea3a0-0252-57e3-aeec-232b99135da4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ meters per second" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262338} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1d0e37-098a-5943-85a3-515d49a429e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e527e4e-1ea7-5d39-a401-6a8d7982a17f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72352" :question/problem "The function $x(t)={t}^{2}+t$ models the distance traveled by a car $t$ seconds after it sets off on a journey. Find the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing with respect to time when $t=1$ second." :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is equal to ${x}^{′}(1).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $x(t)$ with respect to $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)=2t+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $t=1$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(1)=2(1)+1=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the distance traveled by the car is changing when $t=1$ second is $3$ meters per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "236cec41-4836-5526-893d-e6f2a23d1801" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change for a Given Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a52c4689-da7a-570e-8989-65b6edf76e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262352} {:answer/id #uuid "27d04d21-825b-59ef-bea0-e7136ff9cae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{355}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262353} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b91f08-9fb4-5b3f-9b4a-1814f2033caf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$355{e}^{t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262354} {:answer/id #uuid "db14086d-d818-5ca4-a619-9770d3a947e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710{e}^{t}}{2+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262355} {:answer/id #uuid "43741ee7-1f7c-5b81-9f09-2b2714183729" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$710{e}^{-t}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262356}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52c4689-da7a-570e-8989-65b6edf76e48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$  liters per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262352} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fde2b24-adb2-54a1-9c8d-6b66f11b4bbd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1a7b751-463a-5c53-9c04-73eeed9f0b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-86191" :question/problem "The function $P(t)=710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})$ represents the volume of milk, in liters, produced on a farm $t$ days after the owner bought new cows. What is the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk as a function of $t?$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk with respect to time is ${P}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, the rate of change of $P(t)$ with respect to $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(710\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =710\\,\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change of the volume of produced milk is  $\\frac{710}{1+{e}^{-t}}$ liters per day."}]}
